    "created_at": "2020-01-21T21:35:14Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-01-21T21:35:14Z",
    "body": "The precedent here is `timeStamp` being used for \"the time of the occurrence that the event is signaling\" for `input` events. The spec helpfully notes\r\n\r\n> In macOS the time of the occurrence for input actions is available via the timestamp property of NSEvent objects.\r\n\r\nas an example of how this might be determined.\r\n\r\nIs the idea that pageshow as an \"occurrence\" is \"signaled\" when the page comes out of bfcache, and so `timeStamp` should reflect that time? I guess currently `timeStamp` just reflects the time the event was fired... is that significantly different?\r\n\r\nIt seems a little tricky to spec this, given how vague the current spec language around timeStamp is. Maybe we could firm it up by adding a definition around `the time of the occurrence that the event is signaling` in DOM, and having HTML say what that means for pageshow. (Is it possible to say what it means without getting into implementation specifics?)",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
