    "updated_at": "2020-01-22T20:25:34Z",
    "body": "> Is the idea that pageshow as an \"occurrence\" is \"signaled\" when the page comes out of bfcache, and so `timeStamp` should reflect that time? I guess currently `timeStamp` just reflects the time the event was fired... is that significantly different?\r\n\r\nI think they are somewhat different because the current definition would capture the time at which navigation has been committed, which involves some work from the user agent after 'navigation start'. However I do not have data on different these are.\r\n\r\n> \r\n> It seems a little tricky to spec this, given how vague the current spec language around timeStamp is. Maybe we could firm it up by adding a definition around `the time of the occurrence that the event is signaling` in DOM, and having HTML say what that means for pageshow. (Is it possible to say what it means without getting into implementation specifics?)\r\n\r\nIndeed, timeStamp is a bit vague so I'm not sure how to spec this. The first question would be whether others think it makes sense to change the intended meaning of this value.",
